what was your immediate response when  you saw uh the mail on sunday article  well i said it's a great sadness and  obviously you know you can't distract uh  politicians or indeed journalists by  doing it i mean it's farcical isn't yeah  and i think it's great sadness that i'm  just not surprised because this sort of  sexism and misogyny it is frankly the  sort of rubbish that female mps but also  female staffers in the house of commons  have to put up with every single day and  you know when i hear a minister just now  say i haven't heard this sort of thing  before talk to your female colleagues  talk to the women who work in your  office because a lot of them would have  experienced this sort of thing  what then needs to happen next i asked  the minister this morning and frankly  he's perhaps not senior enough to  perhaps be privy to these discussions  but i did ask the minister whether or  not given that given the tone of the  article given the tone of the comments  that we must presume have been made by  by several conservatives that there  shouldn't be some form of internal  investigation  yeah i mean i think that too many people  think this sort of thing is just  acceptable so it's got to be called out  and right from the top and it's got to  be seen as  totally socially unacceptable to be  saying this sort of stuff but you know  there are just too many stories and not  just of of sexism but you know also  things like we've got a conservative mp  in wakefield who's been convicted  of sexual offenses against a child he's  still a member upon when he hasn't had  it in his resignation uh yet you know  we've got to sort out parliament so that  the vetting system of who can be an mp  is improved uh the the processes within  parties improve but also  to make parliament more welcoming for  people including women and women have  been in parliament now for over a  hundred years but still only a third of  mps are women and this sort of rubbish  that angela has been subjected to but  many others as well frankly puts off  good people from putting themselves  forward to parliament and we don't want  that i i certainly don't want this to be  the totality of what we discussed this  morning but one of the curiosities i  just want to show you in the screen of  the way in which the government has  responded to all of this are a couple of  tweets and we put this to chris philp a  little bit earlier here's what the prime  minister uh originally tweeted and if  you can pop it in the screen for us  there we go disagree with angela rainer  on almost every political issue respect  her as a parliamentarian and deplore the  misogyny directed actor anonymously  today and then we heard this  from the culture secretary which if you  squint  is pretty much exactly the same  we understand that boris johnson has  reached out privately to angela raynor  but i wonder what you think this says  about their commitment to sorting this  issue out well this shouldn't just be a  line to take this should be actually  what you feel and what you believe and  also we need some action not just warm  words so the conservative mps have been  briefing journalists this story  need to be made clear to them that this  is totally unacceptable and you know the  conservative mp caroline knox so i've  got huge respect for is calling this out  but is also talking about the culture  that she experiences in parliament  and i think that working together across  the political spectrum we need to do  much more to root out this sort of  behavior as i said i don't want to stay  on this but just just to be absolutely  clear do you think there is a place for  some form of disciplinary action for  those who are responsible for the  briefing well the problem is is we don't  know the names because they don't have  the guts to say this in public they  anonymously brief their friends uh in  the in the press but uh they shouldn't  hide behind that anonymity if that's  what they think have the guts to say it  just to work from you then on what we've  seen across the channel in the past 24  hours emmanuel macron has defeated and  by a margin uh marine le pen who has you  know uh has has acted to soften some of  the more liberal policies that that her  party puts forward but is still broadly  seen as from the far right how concerned  are you  that two in five uh french people who  went to the polls supported her  well it is nice to have some good news  in global politics that the far right in  france have been defeated but no one  should be  relaxed or sanguine about the results as  you say 42 percent of people voted for a  candidate of the uh the far right and  more is needed not just in france but  around the world to tackle some of this  extreme extremism that we see in  politics today from the u.s to france to  sadly britain and beyond i mean there  are there are any number of course of  the places in europe which are also  similarly seeing a rise in far-right  politics i mean is is this what the the  next part of the 21st century is going  to be like a battle against some fairly  illiberal extremist policies i think we  need to take on the the the far right  and uh um marine le pen can uh change  her party's uh name and uh and and try  and put a a a a smile on the far uh  right policies that she espouses but uh  it is uh it is still far right and we  need to to call them out uh for for what  those policies are let's let's talk  about on your brief and  non-dumb status the labour party has  settled on a position let's just hear  what exactly it is if you make britain  your home you should pay your taxes here  that is more than important than ever  because at the moment we are in a cost  of living crisis everybody is noticing  those rising prices and yet at the same  time the conservative party is  increasing taxes on working people and  on the businesses that employ them and  so it is absolutely right that loopholes  are closed down that mean that there are  a privileged few who are getting out of  paying their fair share of taxes these  rules on non-doms are from 200 years ago  they are outdated they are unjustifiable  and unfair and with labour they would go  when you say they would go you you you  do concede i think in the in the press  release that i read that there are a  handful there are small number of cases  in which non-dom status is pertinent is  appropriate so how do you limit it i  mean there appears to be talk of some  kind of time limitation on it so we  would abolish non-dom status but like in  other um modern economies we would have  rules for people who are temporarily in  the uk for a short period of time in  germany and in canada that is six months  in japan it is five years we will work  with business for getting the  appropriate period of time but in the uk  you can be here for year after year  after year  it's time limited to 15. 15. if you live  in the uk for 15 years you should be  paying your taxes here 75 000 people in  the uk are registered for uh non-dom  status uh 2 000 of them have been in the  uk for more than seven years it isn't  right that those people can pay a  one-off fee and get out of paying their  fair share of taxes and so of course we  need to have rules of people who come  here to work on a secondment etc for a  short period of time but if you make  britain your home you should pay your  taxes here like everybody else um so  just in terms of of the time limitation  on this status obviously as you say  you're going to take soundings from  business but it's going to be somewhere  between six months and five years i  would suspect i mean personally where do  you feel is an appropriate place to be  today  i want to consult to get that right but  i've looked at what other countries do  in the u.s you pay full taxes from day  one and no one would suggest that the  u.s is not welcoming of of investment  and entrepreneurs etc so we've got these  different schemes in different countries  we'll consult to get that right but i'm  very clear that the non-dom status that  exists today will not exist with a  labour government we want to ensure that  people feel welcome to come here to to  do a job and to contribute to the  british economy  but that non-dom status where people who  make their home here aren't paying their  taxes that is an outdated concept from  200 years ago and it shouldn't stand  today especially when working people are  seeing their taxes go up and up and up  rachel reeves lovely to see you this  morning thanks for joining us

